[
    {
        "id": "6e6b107ac4a184c5",
        "type": "tab",
        "label": "Kafka 05: consumer control",
        "disabled": false,
        "info": "Pause, resume, stop, start, restart and seek a consumer with `msg.action`, and commit offsets manually after a message was processed (at-least-once processing).",
        "env": []
    },
    {
        "id": "bf1e10cab199ff00",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "6e6b107ac4a184c5",
        "name": "Example 05: consumer control and manual commit - select me and open the Info sidebar",
        "info": "## Consumer control and manual commit\n\n**Before you deploy:** the example connects to a Kafka broker on `localhost:9092`. If yours runs elsewhere, double-click any Kafka node, click the pencil next to **Broker** (the configuration node *Local Kafka (example 05)*) and change **Brokers**. Then click **Deploy**. After the deploy, click **send 5 jobs** to put some messages on the topic `example-work`.\n\n**Topics:** the producer creates `example-work` with its first message if the broker allows automatic topic creation (Apache Kafka does by default, many managed services do not). Until a topic exists, the consumer shows `unknown topic/partition · retry …` (yellow) and keeps retrying, waiting a little longer each time; it starts at the first retry after the topic was created. To create topics yourself, use the admin node (example 06) or your Kafka tools.\n\n### Manual commit: process at least once\n\nKafka remembers, per consumer group and partition, the position of the next message to read (the *committed offset*). With *Commit: Automatically* (the default) the position is stored regularly (every 5 s and after each fetched batch) once the messages were handed to the flow. If Node-RED stops while a message is still being processed, that message is not delivered again (*at most once*); only messages handed over since the last commit are delivered again.\n\nThis consumer uses *Commit: Manually*. Nothing is stored until the processed message comes back to the consumer's input with `msg.action = \"commit\"`: here the *change* node sets it and the *link* nodes carry the message back. The consumer then commits that message's offset + 1, taken from `msg.kafka`, so keep `msg.kafka` on the message. Messages that were not committed are delivered **again** after a restart, a reconnect or a rebalance (*at least once*), so the processing should tolerate duplicates.\n\nCommitting a message also commits every earlier message of the same partition. If processing fails, do not pass the message on (return `null` or throw in the function).\n\nTo see the committed position, use the **group offsets** button of example 06 with the group id `example-work-group` and the topic `example-work`.",
        "x": 380,
        "y": 40,
        "wires": []
    },
    {
        "id": "4ca843ac49a9b868",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "6e6b107ac4a184c5",
        "name": "Control the consumer (msg.action)",
        "info": "- **pause** / **resume**: stop / continue fetching. The consumer stays in its group. A pause is kept across reconnects.\n- **stop**: leave the group and disconnect; the consumer stays stopped until **start**.\n- **restart**: disconnect and connect again now.\n- **seek**: continue reading a partition from another offset: `msg.payload = { topic, partition, offset }`. `topic` can be left out when the consumer has a single topic; `offset` is a number, `\"earliest\"` or `\"latest\"`. Only works while connected.\n\nControl messages are not sent to the output. A failing one (for example *seek* while stopped) goes to the *catch* node.",
        "x": 200,
        "y": 100,
        "wires": []
    },
